IMPORT prelude

-- Family Law Act 1975 (Cth) -- who has responsibility for my child, when may I divorce, and how is property split? -- with the
-- few Rules of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Family Law) Rules 2021 that the separating parent meets.
--
-- SEAT: the separating parent and spouse (Jake). Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it, on facts that person
-- could state. Where a provision addresses only the court or a family dispute resolution practitioner, it is encoded as what it
-- means for the separating parent: whether a divorce order will be made, when it takes effect, whether an application can be
-- filed, what the court must weigh, and by when property proceedings must start.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 39(3) (who may file for divorce in Australia); Part VI: s 48 (irretrievable breakdown: 12 months'
-- separation; no reasonable likelihood of resumed cohabitation), s 49 (separation by one party; separation under one roof),
-- s 50(1) (one resumption of cohabitation of less than 3 months: the periods aggregated), s 52 (a nullity application first),
-- s 55(1), (2), (3), (4) (when the divorce order takes effect: 1 month, the s 55A declaration, an appeal, death), s 55A (the
-- children condition; child of the marriage by treatment), s 59 (marrying again); Part VII: s 60F(1) (child of a marriage, born
-- before or after it), s 69ZE and s 69ZH (how far Part VII reaches a child in Western Australia), s 60CA, s 60CC(1)-(4) (the
-- best-interests considerations), s 60I(7)-(9) (the family dispute resolution certificate and its exemptions), s 61B, s 61C,
-- s 61D(2) (parental responsibility), s 63B (what a parenting plan is worth), s 63C(1), (1A), (2) (what is a parenting plan),
-- s 63D (varying a plan), s 65DAB (the court has regard to the latest plan); property: s 44(3), (4) (12 months after the divorce
-- takes effect; leave), s 79(1)-(2) (the court may alter property interests if just and equitable), s 44(5)(a)(i) and s 90RA
-- (de facto property: 2 years; Western Australia is not a referring State). Rules: r 4.01 (pre-action procedures), r 15.15(2)
-- (attending an undefended divorce hearing).
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 101, compilation date 10 June 2025 (includes amendments up to Act No. 118, 2024), register id
-- C2025C00341, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A00275.txt. Rules: Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia
-- (Family Law) Rules 2021, Compilation No. 4, compilation date 10 June 2025 (includes amendments up to F2025L00638), register id
-- F2025C00594, retrieved 2026-10-19, as deposited at
-- ../../../federal-circuit-and-family-court-of-australia-family-law-rules-2021/registers/source-bundle/F2021L01197.txt. Used:
-- rr 4.01 and 15.15.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the courts, their jurisdiction and transfers (Parts IV, V) beyond s 39(3); family counselling and the duties of
-- advisers (Parts II, III, ss 60D, 63DA); nullity (s 51) beyond s 52; s 50(2) (a cohabitation interruption that is not
-- substantial); rescission of a divorce order (ss 57, 58); parenting orders and their contents (ss 64B-65DA) beyond s 65DAB;
-- the views of the child and independent children's lawyers (s 60CD, Division 10); relocation, recovery, location and
-- contravention (Divisions 6, 8, 13A); child maintenance (Division 7) and child support (the Child Support (Assessment) Act 1989,
-- another subject); spousal maintenance (ss 72-77); the contribution and future-needs considerations of s 79(4)-(5), which the
-- court weighs and the encoding does not; superannuation splitting (Part VIIIB, VIIIC); financial agreements (Part VIIIA);
-- Part VIIIAB beyond its time limit; injunctions, enforcement, offences, regulations. "Domiciled", "ordinarily resident",
-- "cohabitation" and "family violence" are borrowed or factual terms, asked as facts. Every satisfaction of the court is an input.
--
-- WESTERN AUSTRALIA. Property between de facto partners in Western Australia is not split under this Act: s 90RA(3)'s note says
-- "Western Australia is not a referring State", so Part VIIIAB's property orders need a link to a participating jurisdiction.
-- The law that does govern -- the Family Court Act 1997 (WA) -- is named here on the plan's instruction; it is not deposited and
-- was not read.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults: the Act with
-- the Rules the events need; the actors the text names; a judgment the law hands to the court or a practitioner asked as an
-- input, never guessed; the Act's own definitions worked out from facts. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test
-- pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Dates are written YYYYMMDD as numbers. "1 month" and "12 months" are counted as calendar months by moving the month and keeping
-- the day of the month; "12 months immediately preceding the date of the filing" is met when the filing date is on or after the
-- separation date plus 12 months; "within ... 12 months after" a date ends on the same day of the month 12 months later. Those
-- conventions are INFERENCES: the Act does not say how a month is counted, and the Acts Interpretation Act 1901 is not deposited.

§ `Family Law Act 1975`

§§ `dates`

@export The year of a date written YYYYMMDD
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the year of` `d` MEANS FLOOR (`d` DIVIDED BY 10000)

@export The month of a date written YYYYMMDD
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month of` `d` MEANS FLOOR ((`d` MINUS (`the year of` `d` TIMES 10000)) DIVIDED BY 100)

@export The day of the month of a date written YYYYMMDD
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the day of the month of` `d` MEANS `d` MINUS (`the year of` `d` TIMES 10000) MINUS (`the month of` `d` TIMES 100)

@export The same day of the month, a number of calendar months later
GIVEN `n` IS A NUMBER @desc How many calendar months later (not negative)
      `d` IS A NUMBER @desc The starting date, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the date` `n` `months after` `d` MEANS
    ((`the year of` `d` PLUS FLOOR (`t` DIVIDED BY 12)) TIMES 10000) PLUS (((`t` MODULO 12) PLUS 1) TIMES 100) PLUS `the day of the month of` `d`
    WHERE
        `t` MEANS (`the month of` `d` MINUS 1) PLUS `n`

-- A running day count (days since 1 March of year 0 in the proleptic Gregorian calendar), so that two periods can be added.
@export A running day number for a date, for counting days between dates
GIVEN `d` IS A NUMBER @desc A date written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the day number of` `d` MEANS
    (`era` TIMES 146097) PLUS (`yoe` TIMES 365) PLUS FLOOR (`yoe` DIVIDED BY 4) MINUS FLOOR (`yoe` DIVIDED BY 100) PLUS `doy`
    WHERE
        `m` MEANS `the month of` `d`
        `y` MEANS IF `m` AT MOST 2 THEN `the year of` `d` MINUS 1 ELSE `the year of` `d`
        `era` MEANS FLOOR (`y` DIVIDED BY 400)
        `yoe` MEANS `y` MINUS (`era` TIMES 400)
        `mp` MEANS (`m` PLUS 9) MODULO 12
        `doy` MEANS FLOOR (((153 TIMES `mp`) PLUS 2) DIVIDED BY 5) PLUS `the day of the month of` `d` MINUS 1

@export How many days from one date to another
GIVEN `start` IS A NUMBER @desc The earlier date, written YYYYMMDD
      `end` IS A NUMBER @desc The later date, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days from` `start` `until` `end` MEANS `the day number of` `end` MINUS `the day number of` `start`

§ `Part VI -- divorce`

§§ `s 39(3) -- may I file for divorce in Australia`

-- s 39(3): "Proceedings for a divorce order may be instituted under this Act if, at the date on which the application for the
-- order is filed in a court, either party to the marriage: (a) is an Australian citizen; (b) is domiciled in Australia; or (c) is
-- ordinarily resident in Australia and has been so resident for 1 year immediately preceding that date."
-- s 44(1A): proceedings for a divorce order "may be instituted by either party to the marriage or jointly by both parties".
@export May I (alone, or with my spouse jointly) file for divorce in Australia (s 39(3))?
GIVEN `either of us is an Australian citizen` IS A BOOLEAN @desc On the day of filing, you or your spouse is an Australian citizen
      `either of us is domiciled in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc On the day of filing, you or your spouse is domiciled in Australia
      `either of us has been ordinarily resident in Australia for the year before filing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc You or your spouse is ordinarily resident in Australia and has been for the whole year before filing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I file for divorce in Australia` IF
        `either of us is an Australian citizen`
    OR  `either of us is domiciled in Australia`
    OR  `either of us has been ordinarily resident in Australia for the year before filing`

§§ `s 49 -- separation, including under one roof`

-- s 49(1): the parties "may be held to have separated notwithstanding that the cohabitation was brought to an end by the action
-- or conduct of one only of the parties."
-- s 49(2): they "may be held to have separated and to have lived separately and apart notwithstanding that they have continued to
-- reside in the same residence or that either party has rendered some household services to the other."
-- Whether the cohabitation ended is a fact for the court ("cohabitation" is not defined); the two things s 49 says do not stop a
-- separation are taken as facts that do not change the answer.
@export Can we be separated even though we still live under one roof (s 49)?
GIVEN `our married life together ended, by the act of one or both of us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cohabitation came to an end, even if only one of you ended it
      `we still live in the same home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have continued to live in the same residence
      `one of us still does some household things for the other` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether either of you still renders some household services to the other
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are we separated` IF
    `our married life together ended, by the act of one or both of us`

§§ `ss 48(2), 50(1) -- twelve months apart`

-- s 48(2): the ground is established, and the divorce order shall be made, "if, and only if, the court is satisfied that the
-- parties separated and thereafter lived separately and apart for a continuous period of not less than 12 months immediately
-- preceding the date of the filing of the application for the divorce order."
@export From what date may I file for divorce, if we have lived apart since we separated (s 48(2))?
GIVEN `we separated on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date you separated, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The earliest day I may file for divorce` MEANS `the date` 12 `months after` `we separated on`

@export Have we lived apart long enough for me to file on this day (s 48(2))?
GIVEN `we separated on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date you separated, written YYYYMMDD
      `I file on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the divorce application is filed, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have we lived apart long enough to file` IF
    `I file on` AT LEAST `The earliest day I may file for divorce` `we separated on`

-- s 50(1): where the parties "resumed cohabitation on one occasion but, within a period of 3 months after the resumption of
-- cohabitation, they again separated and thereafter lived separately and apart up to the date of the filing of the application,
-- the periods of living separately and apart before and after the period of cohabitation may be aggregated as if they were one
-- continuous period, but the period of cohabitation shall not be deemed to be part of the period of living separately and apart."
-- If the reconciliation lasted longer than that, only the time since the second separation counts (s 48(2) needs a continuous
-- period). Aggregated time is measured in days against the length of the 12 months that began on the first separation.
@export Have we lived apart long enough for me to file, after we got back together once (ss 48(2), 50(1))?
GIVEN `we first separated on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date you first separated, written YYYYMMDD
      `we resumed living together on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date you resumed cohabitation (once only), written YYYYMMDD
      `we separated again on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date you separated again, written YYYYMMDD
      `I file on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the divorce application is filed, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have we lived apart long enough to file after one reconciliation` IF
    IF `we separated again on` AT MOST `the date` 3 `months after` `we resumed living together on`
    THEN     (`the days from` `we first separated on` `until` `we resumed living together on`)
        PLUS (`the days from` `we separated again on` `until` `I file on`)
        AT LEAST `the days from` `we first separated on` `until` (`the date` 12 `months after` `we first separated on`)
    ELSE `Have we lived apart long enough to file` `we separated again on` `I file on`

§§ `ss 48, 52 -- will the court make the divorce order`

-- s 48(1): the application "shall be based on the ground that the marriage has broken down irretrievably."
-- s 48(3): "A divorce order shall not be made if the court is satisfied that there is a reasonable likelihood of cohabitation
-- being resumed."
-- s 52: where a nullity application is also before the court, it "shall not make a divorce order ... unless it has dismissed the
-- application for a decree of nullity".
@export Will the court make our divorce order (ss 39(3), 48, 52)?
GIVEN `I may file for divorce in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to s 39(3) on the day of filing
      `we lived apart for the 12 months before filing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to s 48(2) (with s 50 if you reconciled once)
      `the court is satisfied there is a reasonable likelihood we will live together again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's finding under s 48(3)
      `an undismissed application for nullity of our marriage is also before the court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a nullity application is pending and not dismissed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will the court make our divorce order` IF
        `I may file for divorce in Australia`
    AND `we lived apart for the 12 months before filing`
    AND (NOT `the court is satisfied there is a reasonable likelihood we will live together again`)
    AND (NOT `an undismissed application for nullity of our marriage is also before the court`)

§§ `s 55A -- the children condition`

-- s 55A(1): a divorce order "does not take effect unless the court has, by order, declared that it is satisfied: (a) that there
-- are no children of the marriage who have not attained 18 years of age; or (b) that the only children of the marriage who have
-- not attained 18 years of age are the children specified in the order and that: (i) proper arrangements in all the circumstances
-- have been made for the care, welfare and development of those children; or (ii) there are circumstances by reason of which the
-- divorce order should take effect even though the court is not satisfied that such arrangements have been made."
-- s 55A(3)-(4): for this section a child (including an ex-nuptial child, an adopted child, or a child of neither) "is a child of
-- the marriage if the child was treated by both parties to the marriage as a child of their family" immediately before the
-- parties last separated.
@export Is this child a child of our marriage for the divorce (s 55A(3), (4))?
GIVEN `the child is the child of both of us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is the child of both of you, born before or after the marriage (s 60F(1)(a)), or adopted by you after it (s 60F(1)(b))
      `we both treated the child as a child of our family just before we last separated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether both of you treated the child as a child of your family immediately before your last separation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this child a child of the marriage for s 55A` IF
        `the child is the child of both of us`
    OR  `we both treated the child as a child of our family just before we last separated`

@export Can our divorce take effect, so far as our children go (s 55A(1))?
GIVEN `how many children of the marriage are under 18` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of children of the marriage (s 55A(3)) who have not turned 18
      `the court is satisfied proper arrangements have been made for them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's satisfaction under s 55A(1)(b)(i)
      `the court is satisfied there are circumstances for the divorce to take effect anyway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's satisfaction under s 55A(1)(b)(ii)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can our divorce take effect as to the children` IF
        `how many children of the marriage are under 18` EQUALS 0
    OR  `the court is satisfied proper arrangements have been made for them`
    OR  `the court is satisfied there are circumstances for the divorce to take effect anyway`

§§ `s 55 -- when the divorce takes effect`

-- s 55(1): a divorce order "takes effect by force of this section: (a) at the expiration of a period of 1 month from the making of
-- the order; or (b) from the making of an order under section 55A; whichever is the later."
-- s 55(2): the court may extend the period "having regard to the possibility of an appeal", or reduce it "if it is satisfied that
-- there are special circumstances". The period the court sets is an input (1 month unless changed).
-- s 55(3): if an appeal is instituted before the order takes effect, it takes effect "(a) at the expiration of a period of 1 month
-- from the day on which the appeal is determined or discontinued; or (b) on the day on which the divorce order would have taken
-- effect under subsection (1) if no appeal had been instituted; whichever is the later."
-- s 55(4): "A divorce order does not take effect by force of this section if either of the parties to the marriage has died."
@export Will my divorce take effect at all (s 55(4))?
GIVEN `either of us died before it took effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or your spouse died before the divorce order took effect
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will my divorce take effect` IF
    NOT `either of us died before it took effect`

@export On what date does my divorce take effect (s 55(1)-(3))?
GIVEN `the divorce order was made on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the divorce order, written YYYYMMDD
      `the s 55A declaration was made on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the court's s 55A declaration about the children, written YYYYMMDD (often the same day)
      `months set by the court` IS A NUMBER @desc The period before the order takes effect, in months: 1 unless the court extended or reduced it under s 55(2)
      `an appeal was instituted before it took effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an appeal (in the s 55(5) sense) was instituted before the order took effect
      `the appeal was determined or discontinued on` IS A NUMBER @desc If there was an appeal, the date it was determined or discontinued, written YYYYMMDD (otherwise any number)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `When does my divorce take effect` IS
    IF `an appeal was instituted before it took effect`
    THEN `the later of` (`the date` 1 `months after` `the appeal was determined or discontinued on`) `and` `the subsection (1) date`
    ELSE `the subsection (1) date`
    WHERE
        `the subsection (1) date` MEANS `the later of` (`the date` `months set by the court` `months after` `the divorce order was made on`) `and` `the s 55A declaration was made on`
        `the later of` `a` `and` `b` MEANS IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

-- s 59: "If a divorce order under this Act in relation to a marriage has taken effect, a party to the marriage may marry again."
@export May I marry again on this day (s 59)?
GIVEN `my divorce takes effect on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the divorce order takes effect, written YYYYMMDD
      `the day` IS A NUMBER @desc The day you would marry, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I marry again` IF
    `the day` AT LEAST `my divorce takes effect on`

§§ `Rules r 4.01, r 15.15 -- before and at the divorce hearing`

-- r 4.01(1): "before starting a proceeding, each prospective party to the proceeding must comply with the pre-action procedures."
-- r 4.01(2): not necessary if "(a) the proceeding is an application for divorce only", "(c) the proceeding is a child support
-- application or appeal", or "(f) the court is satisfied that, in the circumstances, it was not appropriate for a party to
-- comply". r 4.01(3) lists circumstances the court may count: allegations or risk of child abuse or family violence, urgency,
-- undue prejudice, a previous application in the same cause in the past 12 months.
DECLARE `Proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `an application for divorce only`
    `a parenting proceeding`
    `a financial or property proceeding`
    `a child support application or appeal`

@export Must I follow the court's pre-action procedures before I file (Rules r 4.01)?
GIVEN `my proceeding` IS A `Proceeding` @desc What you are applying for
      `the court is satisfied it was not appropriate for me to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's satisfaction under r 4.01(2)(f), for example because of family violence or urgency (r 4.01(3))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I follow the pre-action procedures` IS
    CONSIDER `my proceeding`
    WHEN `an application for divorce only` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a child support application or appeal` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOT `the court is satisfied it was not appropriate for me to comply`

-- r 15.15(1): "Unless the court otherwise directs, a party and the party's lawyer (if any) must attend each court event."
-- r 15.15(2): that does not apply to "(b) an Application for Divorce if: (i) the application was made jointly by the parties; and
-- (ii) both applicants have requested the court to determine the proceedings in the absence of the parties; or (ba) an
-- Application for Divorce if: (i) the application was made by one party to the marriage; and (ii) the applicant has requested the
-- court to determine the proceedings in the absence of the parties; and (iii) the respondent has not requested to attend".
@export Must I attend the hearing of my divorce application (Rules r 15.15)?
GIVEN `we applied jointly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the divorce application was made by both of you jointly
      `I asked for it to be decided without us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the applicant) asked the court to decide it in the parties' absence
      `my spouse asked for it to be decided without us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a joint application, whether your spouse made the same request
      `my spouse as respondent asked to attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a sole application, whether the respondent asked to attend the hearing
      `the court directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed that you need not attend
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I attend my divorce hearing` IF
        (NOT `the court directed otherwise`)
    AND (NOT `the exception applies`)
    WHERE
        `the exception applies` MEANS
            IF `we applied jointly`
            THEN `I asked for it to be decided without us` AND `my spouse asked for it to be decided without us`
            ELSE `I asked for it to be decided without us` AND (NOT `my spouse as respondent asked to attend`)

§ `Part VII -- children`

§§ `ss 60F, 69ZE, 69ZH -- does Part VII reach my child`

-- s 60F(1): "a child is ... a child of a marriage if: (a) the child is the child of both parties to the marriage, whether born
-- before or after the marriage; or (b) the child is adopted after the marriage by both parties".
@export Is my child a child of our marriage (s 60F(1))?
GIVEN `the child is the child of both of us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is the child of both of you, born before or after the marriage
      `we both adopted the child after we married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was adopted after the marriage by both of you (or one with the consent of the other)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my child a child of our marriage` IF
        `the child is the child of both of us`
    OR  `we both adopted the child after we married`

-- s 69ZE(1): Part VII "extends to New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia and Tasmania". (2): it "extends to
-- Western Australia if: (a) the Parliament of Western Australia refers" the matters "or (b) Western Australia adopts this Part."
-- s 69ZH(2)-(3): the parenting provisions also have effect as if confined to "a child of a marriage" and "the parties to the
-- marriage", so far as they deal with "the parental responsibility of the parties to a marriage for a child of the marriage".
-- Whether Western Australia has referred or adopted Part VII is not in the deposit; it is not answered here. Part VII's reach
-- in a Territory is not stated in Subdivision F; that it applies there is an INFERENCE.
DECLARE `Where we live` IS ONE OF
    `Western Australia`
    `New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia or Tasmania`
    `a Territory`

DECLARE `Reach of Part VII` IS ONE OF
    `yes, Part VII extends to this State (s 69ZE(1))`
    `yes, through the marriage (s 69ZH)`
    `yes, in a Territory (an inference)`
    `only if Western Australia has referred or adopted Part VII (s 69ZE(2)), which the deposit does not show`

@export Does Part VII of this Act govern parenting of my child where we live (ss 69ZE, 69ZH)?
GIVEN `my child is a child of our marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to s 60F(1)
      `we live in` IS A `Where we live` @desc The State or Territory
GIVETH A `Reach of Part VII`
DECIDE `Does Part VII reach my child` IS
    CONSIDER `we live in`
    WHEN `New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia or Tasmania` THEN `yes, Part VII extends to this State (s 69ZE(1))`
    WHEN `a Territory` THEN `yes, in a Territory (an inference)`
    WHEN `Western Australia` THEN
        IF `my child is a child of our marriage`
        THEN `yes, through the marriage (s 69ZH)`
        ELSE `only if Western Australia has referred or adopted Part VII (s 69ZE(2)), which the deposit does not show`

§§ `ss 61B, 61C, 61D -- parental responsibility`

-- s 61B: parental responsibility "means all the duties, powers, responsibilities and authority which, by law, parents have in
-- relation to children."
-- s 61C(1): "Each of the parents of a child who is not 18 has parental responsibility for the child." (2): this "has effect despite
-- any changes in the nature of the relationships of the child's parents. It is not affected, for example, by the parents becoming
-- separated". (3): it is subject to any court order.
-- s 61D(2): a parenting order does not take away any aspect of parental responsibility "except to the extent (if any): (a)
-- expressly provided for in the order; or (b) necessary to give effect to the order."
@export Do I have parental responsibility for my child, now that we have separated (ss 61B-61D)?
GIVEN `I am a parent of the child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the child's parent
      `the age of the child` IS A NUMBER @desc The child's age in completed years
      `a court order takes it away from me, expressly or as needed to give effect to the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parenting or other court order removes your parental responsibility (s 61D(2))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have parental responsibility for my child` IF
        `I am a parent of the child`
    AND `the age of the child` LESS THAN 18
    AND (NOT `a court order takes it away from me, expressly or as needed to give effect to the order`)

§§ `ss 60CA, 60CC -- the best interests of the child`

-- s 60CA: "In deciding whether to make a particular parenting order in relation to a child, a court must regard the best interests
-- of the child as the paramount consideration."
-- s 60CC(2): the court "must consider" (a) arrangements that promote safety (including from family violence, abuse, neglect) of
-- the child and each carer; (b) any views expressed by the child; (c) the child's developmental, psychological, emotional and
-- cultural needs; (d) each person's capacity to provide for those needs; (e) the benefit of a relationship with the parents and
-- other significant people, "where it is safe to do so"; (f) "anything else that is relevant". (2A): safety includes any history
-- of family violence, abuse or neglect and any family violence order. (3): for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander child,
-- also (a) the right to enjoy that culture and (b) the likely impact of the order on that right.
-- s 60CC(4): for an order made with the consent of all the parties, the court "may, but is not required to, have regard to" them.
@export How many best-interests matters must the court consider for my child (s 60CC(1)-(3))?
GIVEN `my child is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander child
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many best interests matters must the court consider` IS
    IF `my child is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander child` THEN 8 ELSE 6

DECLARE `Weight of the s 60CC matters` IS ONE OF
    `the court must consider them`
    `the court may, but need not, consider them`

@export If we ask for parenting orders by consent, must the court still weigh the best-interests matters (s 60CC(4))?
GIVEN `all the parties consent to the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order is sought with the consent of all the parties
GIVETH A `Weight of the s 60CC matters`
DECIDE `Must the court weigh the best interests matters` IS
    IF `all the parties consent to the order` THEN `the court may, but need not, consider them` ELSE `the court must consider them`

§§ `s 60I -- family dispute resolution before a parenting application`

-- s 60I(7): "An application for a Part VII order in relation to a child must not be accepted by the court for filing unless: (a)
-- the applicant files ... a certificate given to the applicant by a family dispute resolution practitioner under subsection (8);
-- or (b) after the making of the application, the court grants the applicant an exemption under subsection (8A)".
-- s 60I(8): the five kinds of certificate: (a) the other party refused or failed to attend; (aa) the practitioner considers it
-- would not be appropriate to conduct it; (b) all attended and made a genuine effort; (c) someone did not make a genuine effort;
-- (d) it began but it would not be appropriate to continue. Any of the five lets the application be filed.
DECLARE `FDR certificate` IS ONE OF
    `no certificate`
    `s 60I(8)(a): the other party refused or failed to attend`
    `s 60I(8)(aa): not appropriate to conduct`
    `s 60I(8)(b): we attended and all made a genuine effort`
    `s 60I(8)(c): we attended but someone did not make a genuine effort`
    `s 60I(8)(d): begun, but not appropriate to continue`

@export Will the court accept my parenting application for filing (s 60I(7))?
GIVEN `my certificate` IS A `FDR certificate` @desc The family dispute resolution certificate you have, if any
      `the court granted me an exemption` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has granted you an exemption under s 60I(8A)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will my parenting application be accepted for filing` IS
    CONSIDER `my certificate`
    WHEN `no certificate` THEN `the court granted me an exemption`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 60I(8B): the court may grant the exemption "only if the court is satisfied that one or more of the grounds in subsection (9)
-- exist": (a) consent orders, or a response to another party's application; (b) reasonable grounds to believe there has been, or
-- would be a risk of, child abuse or family violence by a party; (c) a contravention, within 12 months of an order on the same
-- issue, showing "a serious disregard"; (d) urgency; (e) a party "unable to participate effectively"; (f) circumstances in the
-- regulations (not deposited, so not offered).
DECLARE `Exemption ground` IS ONE OF
    `none of the grounds`
    `the order is sought by consent of all the parties`
    `I am responding to the other party's application`
    `reasonable grounds to believe there has been or is a risk of child abuse or family violence`
    `a serious contravention of an order on the same issue made in the last 12 months`
    `urgency`
    `a party cannot participate effectively in family dispute resolution`

@export Could the court exempt me from needing a family dispute resolution certificate (s 60I(8A), (8B), (9))?
GIVEN `the ground I rely on` IS A `Exemption ground` @desc The s 60I(9) ground you rely on
      `the court is satisfied the ground exists` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's satisfaction under s 60I(8B)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the court exempt me from the certificate` IS
    CONSIDER `the ground I rely on`
    WHEN `none of the grounds` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `the court is satisfied the ground exists`

§§ `ss 63B-63D, 65DAB -- the parenting plan`

-- s 63C(1): "A parenting plan is an agreement that: (a) is in writing; and (b) is or was made between the parents of a child; and
-- (ba) is signed by the parents of the child; and (bb) is dated; and (c) deals with a matter or matters mentioned in subsection
-- (2)." (1A): "An agreement is not a parenting plan for the purposes of this Act unless it is made free from any threat, duress or
-- coercion." (2): the matters include with whom the child lives, time with others, the allocation of parental responsibility,
-- communication, maintenance, dispute resolution, and "any aspect of the care, welfare or development of the child".
@export Is our agreement about our child a parenting plan under the Act (s 63C(1), (1A))?
GIVEN `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement is in writing
      `it was made between the parents of the child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is or was made between the child's parents
      `both parents signed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is signed by the parents
      `it is dated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is dated
      `it deals with a s 63C(2) matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it deals with where the child lives, time with others, parental responsibility, communication or another s 63C(2) matter
      `it was made free from threat, duress or coercion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made free from any threat, duress or coercion
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is our agreement a parenting plan` IF
        `it is in writing`
    AND `it was made between the parents of the child`
    AND `both parents signed it`
    AND `it is dated`
    AND `it deals with a s 63C(2) matter`
    AND `it was made free from threat, duress or coercion`

-- s 63B note: "Parents who seek enforceable arrangements require court orders. These can be obtained by consent."
-- s 65DAB: "When making a parenting order in relation to a child, the court is to have regard to the terms of the most recent
-- parenting plan (if any) that has been entered into between the child's parents ... if doing so would be in the best interests of
-- the child."
DECLARE `Worth of the plan` IS ONE OF
    `a parenting plan: not enforceable as an order, but the court has regard to the latest plan when making a parenting order, if that is in the child's best interests`
    `not a parenting plan under the Act`

@export What is our parenting plan worth (ss 63B, 65DAB)?
GIVEN `it is a parenting plan` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to s 63C
GIVETH A `Worth of the plan`
DECIDE `What is our parenting plan worth` IS
    IF `it is a parenting plan`
    THEN `a parenting plan: not enforceable as an order, but the court has regard to the latest plan when making a parenting order, if that is in the child's best interests`
    ELSE `not a parenting plan under the Act`

-- s 63D: "A parenting plan, other than a plan to which section 63DB applies, may be varied or revoked by agreement in writing
-- between the parties to the plan." (s 63DB, registered plans from before 2003, is not encoded.)
@export May we change or cancel our parenting plan (s 63D)?
GIVEN `we both agree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties to the plan agree
      `the agreement is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the variation or revocation is in writing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May we change our parenting plan` IF
    `we both agree` AND `the agreement is in writing`

§ `Part VIII -- property`

§§ `s 44(3), (4) -- the 12 months after the divorce`

-- s 44(3): after a divorce order has taken effect, property and maintenance proceedings "shall not be instituted, except by leave
-- of the court ... or with the consent of both of the parties to the marriage, after the expiration of 12 months after: (c) ... the
-- date on which the divorce order took effect".
-- s 44(4): the court "shall not grant leave ... unless it is satisfied: (a) that hardship would be caused to a party to the
-- relevant marriage or a child if leave were not granted; or (b)" for maintenance, that the applicant could not then have
-- supported himself or herself without an income tested pension, allowance or benefit.
@export What is the last day I can start property proceedings without leave or consent (s 44(3))?
GIVEN `my divorce took effect on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the divorce order took effect, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The last day to start property proceedings without leave` MEANS `the date` 12 `months after` `my divorce took effect on`

@export May I start property settlement proceedings on this day (s 44(3))?
GIVEN `our divorce has taken effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a divorce order has taken effect (before then s 44(3) sets no limit)
      `my divorce took effect on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the divorce order took effect, written YYYYMMDD
      `the day` IS A NUMBER @desc The day you would start the proceedings, written YYYYMMDD
      `we both consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether both of you consent to the proceedings being instituted
      `the court grants me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court grants leave (see s 44(4))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I start property proceedings` IF
        (NOT `our divorce has taken effect`)
    OR  `the day` AT MOST `The last day to start property proceedings without leave` `my divorce took effect on`
    OR  `we both consent`
    OR  `the court grants me leave`

@export Could the court give me leave to start property proceedings late (s 44(4)(a))?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied hardship would be caused to me, my spouse or a child without leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's satisfaction under s 44(4)(a)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the court give me leave to start late` IF
    `the court is satisfied hardship would be caused to me, my spouse or a child without leave`

§§ `s 79(1), (2) -- will the court alter our property interests`

-- s 79(1): in property settlement proceedings "the court may ... make such order as it considers appropriate ... altering the
-- interests of the parties to the marriage in the property".
-- s 79(2): "The court must not make an order under this section unless it is satisfied that, in all the circumstances, it is just
-- and equitable to make the order."
-- s 79(3)-(5): the court identifies the property and liabilities and takes into account contributions and current and future
-- circumstances. Those are the court's weighing and are NOT encoded; the outcome is asked.
-- That there must be some property of either party for an order to alter is an INFERENCE from "altering the interests ... in the
-- property"; the Act does not say so in terms.
@export Will the court alter our property interests (s 79(1), (2))?
GIVEN `I may start the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The answer to s 44(3)
      `there is property of either of us to alter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether either of you has any property (an inference: with none there is nothing to alter)
      `the court is satisfied an order is just and equitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's satisfaction under s 79(2)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will the court alter our property interests` IF
        `I may start the proceedings`
    AND `there is property of either of us to alter`
    AND `the court is satisfied an order is just and equitable`

§§ `ss 44(5), 90RA -- which law splits our property`

-- s 90RA(1): "the participating jurisdictions" are "(a) each referring State; (b) each Territory." Note to s 90RA(3): "Western
-- Australia is not a referring State."
-- s 90SK and s 90SD: a de facto property or maintenance order needs either party ordinarily resident in a participating
-- jurisdiction when applying, and a link (a third of the relationship there, or substantial contributions there). The link is
-- asked as one fact.
-- s 44(5)(a)(i): a de facto party may apply "within the period (the standard application period) of: (i) 2 years after the end of
-- the de facto relationship", or (b) with the consent of both.
DECLARE `Our relationship` IS ONE OF
    `married`
    `de facto`

DECLARE `Property law` IS ONE OF
    `this Act: property settlement under s 79`
    `this Act: Part VIIIAB, de facto property`
    `not this Act: in Western Australia, the Family Court Act 1997 (WA)`
    `not this Act: no link to a participating jurisdiction`

@export Which law splits our property after we separate (s 79, s 90RA, s 90SK)?
GIVEN `we were` IS A `Our relationship` @desc Married or in a de facto relationship
      `we live in` IS A `Where we live` @desc The State or Territory
      `we meet the s 90SK link to a participating jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a de facto couple: either of you ordinarily resident in a referring State or Territory when applying, and the s 90SK(1)(b) link met
GIVETH A `Property law`
DECIDE `Which law splits our property` IS
    CONSIDER `we were`
    WHEN `married` THEN `this Act: property settlement under s 79`
    WHEN `de facto` THEN
        IF `we meet the s 90SK link to a participating jurisdiction`
        THEN `this Act: Part VIIIAB, de facto property`
        ELSE IF `we live in` EQUALS `Western Australia`
             THEN `not this Act: in Western Australia, the Family Court Act 1997 (WA)`
             ELSE `not this Act: no link to a participating jurisdiction`

@export For a de facto couple under this Act: the last day to apply for property orders without leave (s 44(5)(a)(i))
GIVEN `our relationship ended on` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the de facto relationship ended, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The last day to apply for de facto property orders` MEANS `the date` 24 `months after` `our relationship ended on`
